# BeanPool Environment Variables
# Copy to .env and fill in your values

CF_API_TOKEN=your-cloudflare-api-token
CF_ZONE_ID=your-cloudflare-zone-id

# Used for Universal Links in the apple-app-site-association file
# Get this 10-character code from the Apple Developer Portal -> Membership Details
APPLE_TEAM_ID=your-10-char-apple-team-id

# --- Federation (#143) --------------------------------------------------------
# Both OFF unless explicitly set to the string "true". Set these in a NODE'S OWN
# .env on the box (deploy.sh preserves it across redeploys), not here — federation
# is opted into per node, not fleet-wide.
#
#   ENABLE_PEER_CONNECTORS  this node talks to peer nodes at all
#   FEDERATION_SETTLEMENT   this node accepts NEW cross-node trades. Turning it
#                           back off still completes trades already in flight, so
#                           it never strands committed beans.
ENABLE_PEER_CONNECTORS=
FEDERATION_SETTLEMENT=

# --- Node profile ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave NODE_PROFILE empty for a community node (local). "global" is for the
# worldwide lobby; the switches it flips are in apps/server/src/config/node-profile.ts,
# and /api/community/info reports which of them this build really does. Set it in
# the node's own .env: it is read at every boot, never taken from the database.
# A database that is a global node (restored, or taken over by a standby) refuses
# to start under any other value; NODE_PROFILE_ALLOW_CHANGE_FROM=global for one
# start converts it on purpose. MIN_APP_VERSION raises the oldest app build this
# node accepts without a banner; empty keeps the built-in floor. From the app build
# with the full-screen update, an app below the floor also stops with "Update
# required" at its next start, once MIN_APP_VERSION_FROM (a date, e.g. 2026-10-15;
# empty = already) has passed, and only once its store has a build that meets the
# floor. MIN_APP_VERSION_IOS / _ANDROID and MIN_APP_VERSION_FROM_IOS / _ANDROID set
# one platform's own. Builds from before the full-screen update can't say their
# platform: their banner reads the lower of the two platforms' floors. The operator
# manual's "Raising the app's floor" says how.
# The global node sets MIN_APP_VERSION to the first app build whose welcome screen
# has "Explore BeanPool worldwide" (G7; after 1.2.55, which does not), once that
# build is in both stores: an older build that reaches the global node then shows
# the update banner rather than a lobby it can't join. An ops step, not code.
NODE_PROFILE=
NODE_PROFILE_ALLOW_CHANGE_FROM=
# Private preview: 1 = only invites made by the owner or an admin join, visitors see nothing (empty = off;
# any value but 1/true/yes/on or 0/false/no/off stops the node at start).
PRIVATE_PREVIEW=
MIN_APP_VERSION=
MIN_APP_VERSION_IOS=
MIN_APP_VERSION_ANDROID=
MIN_APP_VERSION_FROM=
MIN_APP_VERSION_FROM_IOS=
MIN_APP_VERSION_FROM_ANDROID=

# --- Live-updates socket caps (apps/server/src/ws-limits.ts) ---------------------
# Leave empty for the profile's own. A community (local): 2,000 sockets on the
# node, 1,000 of them strangers' (no member behind them), 64 from one address,
# 8 strangers' from one address, 8 for one member. The global node, sized for a
# 1 GB server: 5,000, 1,500, 1,000, 8 and 4. A socket takes about 10 KB, so scale
# with the server's memory: WS_MAX_SOCKETS=20000 on 4 GB. A whole number above 0,
# or the line is ignored and the boot log says so. Strangers never get more than
# the node has. The listeners' connection cap follows these.
WS_MAX_SOCKETS=
WS_MAX_STRANGER_SOCKETS=
WS_MAX_SOCKETS_PER_ADDRESS=
WS_MAX_STRANGER_SOCKETS_PER_ADDRESS=
WS_MAX_SOCKETS_PER_MEMBER=

# --- Heavy list reads (apps/server/src/heavy-reads.ts) ----------------------------
# How many MB of heavy list answers (the whole member list, the web app's People
# list, a big group's roster) may be in flight at once. Past it a reader waits a
# few seconds, then is told "busy" (503) and asks again later, so a burst can't
# run the server out of memory. Leave empty for 48: about four member lists of
# 30,000, sized for the default 512 MB heap on a 1 GB server. A whole number
# above 0; raise it with the heap on a bigger server. Anything else is ignored,
# with a warning in the log.
HEAVY_READ_BUDGET_MB=

# --- The update check -----------------------------------------------------------
# Empty: the server asks GitHub for the newest BeanPool release 30 s after it
# starts and every 6 hours, so Settings can say whether an update is out.
# true: it never asks on its own. Nothing else depends on it, and the "check now"
# button in Settings still asks when an owner or admin presses it.
DISABLE_UPDATE_CHECK=

# --- Key vault tickets at the open door (BeanPool's global node only) ------------
# Leave empty on every other node: its open door, if it has one, takes only its
# own nonce, exactly as before, and needs nothing of BeanPool's.
# On the global node: the key vault's PUBLIC ticket keys, comma-separated, newest
# first, at most two, each 64 lower-case hex characters. The same value as the
# app build's EXPO_PUBLIC_BEANPOOL_VAULT_TICKET_KEYS, from the vault's genesis.
# With them, POST /api/join also takes a sign-in bound to a key vault deposit
# ticket, checked offline with these keys (the vault is never asked), and the
# door's nonce answer lists them so phones know. Malformed = none, and the boot
# log says so. Public, from this file only: never stored, never in a backup or a
# standby's copy. Give a standby the same line. A test vault's keys until the
# real vault's genesis; then the real ones, at the same time as the app build.
BEANPOOL_VAULT_TICKET_KEYS=

# --- Image store (storage design §7) -------------------------------------------
# Where photos and message attachments live. Leave IMAGE_STORE unset for the
# default: on this node's own disk, beside state.db (data/images). Nothing to
# sign up for; that is what every local node uses.
#
# IMAGE_STORE=s3 keeps them in an S3-compatible bucket instead (Cloudflare R2
# first; the global node). All five IMAGE_S3_* values are then required, from
# the environment only: they are never stored in node_config and never put in
# a backup. The node REFUSES TO START if any is missing, if the bucket cannot
# be reached with them, on a standby, or while this node still has photos on
# its own disk (moving a node from disk to a bucket needs the migration tool,
# which is not in this version).
#
#   IMAGE_S3_ENDPOINT           https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com  (no path)
#   IMAGE_S3_BUCKET             the bucket name
#   IMAGE_S3_REGION             auto  (for R2)
#   IMAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID      an R2 API token's access key id (read + write on that bucket)
#   IMAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY  its secret
IMAGE_STORE=
IMAGE_S3_ENDPOINT=
IMAGE_S3_BUCKET=
IMAGE_S3_REGION=
IMAGE_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
IMAGE_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# --- Backups off the server (optional) ------------------------------------------
# The main server can send its LOCKED backups, on a schedule, to S3-compatible
# storage you choose (Cloudflare R2, Backblaze B2, Wasabi, AWS S3, a MinIO of
# your own...). Nothing is sent anywhere unless you set a destination, here or
# in Settings (Appliance & Data, Backups & Restore). None is ours and none is
# needed. Only locked backups leave the server: with no recovery code nothing
# is sent, and Settings says why. A standby sends nothing.
#
# Up to two destinations here (more in Settings, five in all). For each, all of
# ENDPOINT, BUCKET, REGION, ACCESS_KEY_ID and SECRET_ACCESS_KEY; NAME and PREFIX
# (a folder in the bucket) are optional. From the environment only: never in
# the database, a backup or the log. Use a key that may read, write, list and
# delete in that one bucket.
#
#   BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_ENDPOINT           https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com  (no path; https only)
#   BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_BUCKET             the bucket name
#   BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_REGION             auto  (for R2; your provider's region elsewhere)
#   BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_PREFIX             optional folder, e.g. beanpool
#   BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_ACCESS_KEY_ID      the key's id
#   BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY  its secret
#   BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_NAME               optional label for Settings, e.g. Cloudflare R2
#   (the same with BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_ for a second one)
#
# BACKUP_OFFBOX_INTERVAL_HOURS: hours between backups, 1 to 168. Empty = 24.
# BACKUP_OFFBOX_RETENTION_DAYS: days each is kept there, 1 to 30. Empty = 30,
# and never more: a member who deletes their account is in every backup made
# before. Both can be changed in Settings, which then wins.
BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_BUCKET=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_REGION=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_PREFIX=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_1_NAME=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_ENDPOINT=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_BUCKET=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_REGION=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_PREFIX=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_2_NAME=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_INTERVAL_HOURS=
BACKUP_OFFBOX_RETENTION_DAYS=

# Your community's address, <name>.beanpool.org, claimed by this server with its own key and kept fresh (optional).
# `beanpool claim --name <name>` does the same once, at install. Leave empty for your own domain or none.
# PUBLIC_ADDRESS_NAME=
